        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{-1}^{3}\\frac{x}{4 x^{2} + 4}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x^{2} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8 x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =8 x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x^{2} + 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 3 \\\\ u & 8 & 40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{3}\\frac{x}{4 x^{2} + 4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{8}^{40}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{8}^{40}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int_{8}^{40}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\ln(u)|_{8}^{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\ln(40)-\\ln(8)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\log{\\left(5 \\right)}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
